#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devloop-auto-mode-rule: the rule for the classifier of auto mode, read or
entered in the user's settings file.

The rule is the wording decided on 23 October 2026, the roadmap entry of that
date, in English, word for word, one entry of the list autoMode.allow in the
settings file of the user: ~/.claude/settings.json, or settings.json under
CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR where that is set - the settings page of Claude Code,
"Configuration directories", read on 9 October 2026: "To keep the
home-directory files somewhere else, set CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR; Claude Code then
stores your settings, session history, and plugins there instead." The page
"Configure auto mode", read the same day, gives autoMode.allow as an array of
prose descriptions read as natural-language rules, with the literal string
"$defaults" keeping the built-in rules beside them. Until 23 October 2026 the
rule was text 4 of the roadmap entry of 9 October 2026, which named five
command-line tools and three browser installers; that wording stands below as
the earlier one. Where it stands word for word under autoMode.allow, the rule
counts as not standing, since the new one allows more, and write replaces it
in its place, without a second entry beside it.

The ways of the rule are the commands its list names: the eleven routes whose
place the install guard, hooks/pre-tool-use-install-guard.sh, asks on the
machine, the names a record can carry under install-route, and the three
browser installers, each in the spelling the rule writes; decided on 23
October 2026. The self-test holds three sets equal - the ways in the wording
of the rule, the routes and browser installers in the source of the guard,
and the ways the guard records under PermissionDenied when it runs - so that
a way added in one place and missing in another makes it red. The other forms
the guard reads as an install are no ways of the rule, and auto mode judges
them by itself.

Usage
    bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule read     says whether the rule stands; writes
                                        nothing. Exit 0 where it stands, 1 where
                                        it does not, 2 where the file cannot be
                                        read for it
    bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule write    enters the rule, after the person's yes
                                        to the question on it - in setup-checks
                                        step 8, or at a start with --auto after
                                        a refusal of auto mode, which
                                        bin/devloop-auto-mode-question decides
                                        since 22 October 2026 - and never
                                        without it. Exit 0 where it stands
                                        afterwards, 2 where nothing was written
    bin/devloop-auto-mode-rule --self-test
                                        every outcome of read and write once,
                                        under a CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR of its own,
                                        and the ways held equal; since 23
                                        October 2026

What write does. Where the file or the list is missing, the list is created,
with "$defaults" before the rule; where a list stands, the rule is appended to
it, without "$defaults"; where the earlier wording stands in it, the rule
takes its place, and a second copy of the earlier wording further down the
list goes; where the rule stands already, nothing changes. Every
other key and value of the file stays as it was, in the order it had; the file
is written through a temporary file in the directory of the file itself and
moved over the old one, so that no half file is ever left. Where settings.json
is a symbolic link, the file it points to is written, through every link on the
way, and the link stays as it is: the temporary file is made in the directory
of that file, so that the move stays on one file system, and that file keeps
its permissions. A link to a file that does not exist yet, in a directory that
does, has the file made there, and the link stays. Where that directory does
not exist either, nothing is written and no directory is made, since one could
stand in the way of a repository yet to be fetched to that place; the reason is
printed, and the answer is the one for a refused write. Until 10 October 2026
the temporary file was made beside the link and moved over the link itself,
which left a plain file in its place and the file it pointed to unchanged,
measured that day, the addendum of the roadmap entry of 9 October 2026; the
changelog of Claude Code names such links as a setup it serves, under 2.1.181,
a relative link under a linked ~/.claude, and under 2.1.247, a link managed by
nix, home-manager or stow. A hard link is not kept, as before: the other name
keeps the old content. Reading follows a link as any open does. Where the file
is not valid JSON, holds a key twice, autoMode is not an object or
autoMode.allow is not a list, nothing is written and the reason is printed,
exit 2; the same where the write itself is refused. Both ways print one line on
stdout and exit as above.

It needs python3 and nothing else; the self-test needs git and bash as well,
for the guard it runs. On a Mac, python3 comes with the Command
Line Tools that bring git, so a machine this plugin runs on has it, and the
stock-take tool and the check under "Before you change anything, run this" in
docs/skill-conventions.md rest on it already; jq is not on a Mac by default.
Measured on 9 October 2026 with HOME in a throwaway directory, the roadmap
entry of that day, and on 10 October 2026 through links, its addendum of that
day. The self-test, since 23 October 2026, runs every outcome on every system
the job hooks on every system in .github/workflows/stock-take.yml runs on; a
symbolic link where the system lets it make one, and a line saying so where
it does not; until that day four of the outcomes ran there inside the
self-test of bin/devloop-auto-mode-question and the rest on this Mac alone.
"""

import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import stat
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

RULE = (
    "Installing or updating a tool or a browser for tests that the current "
    "task needs is allowed in a repository set up with devloop, by these "
    "commands: brew install, go install, npm install -g, pnpm add -g, yarn "
    "global add, bun add -g, cargo install, gem install, pipx install, uv tool "
    "install and pip install, and playwright install, cypress install or "
    "puppeteer browsers install for a browser. A devloop hook reads the "
    "project's install permission and where each such command installs before "
    "it runs, and blocks it where the person has not said yes or where it "
    "installs to a place the permission does not name."
)
# The earlier wordings, each of which counts as no rule and is replaced by
# write: text 4 of the roadmap entry of 9 October 2026, until 23 October 2026.
EARLIER = (
    "Installing a tool that the current task needs is allowed in a repository "
    "set up with devloop: a command-line tool with brew install, go install, "
    "cargo install, uv tool install or pipx install, and a browser for tests "
    "with playwright install, cypress install or puppeteer browsers install. "
    "A devloop hook reads the project's install permission before every such "
    "command and blocks it where the person has not said yes.",
)
DEFAULTS = "$defaults"
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
GUARD = os.path.join(HERE, "..", "hooks", "pre-tool-use-install-guard.sh")


def settings_path():
    base = os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") or os.path.join(
        os.path.expanduser("~"), ".claude")
    return os.path.join(base, "settings.json")


def no_duplicate_keys(pairs):
    seen = set()
    for key, _ in pairs:
        if key in seen:
            raise ValueError("the key %r stands twice in one object" % key)
        seen.add(key)
    return dict(pairs)


def load(path):
    """Returns (data, state): data the parsed file or None where it is missing;
    state one of "missing", "no autoMode", "no allow", "standing", "earlier",
    "absent". Raises ValueError with the reason where the file cannot be read
    for the rule."""
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return None, "missing"
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        text = f.read()
    try:
        data = json.loads(text, object_pairs_hook=no_duplicate_keys)
    except ValueError as e:
        raise ValueError("%s is not valid JSON: %s" % (path, e))
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        raise ValueError("%s does not hold an object at its top" % path)
    if "autoMode" not in data:
        return data, "no autoMode"
    auto = data["autoMode"]
    if not isinstance(auto, dict):
        raise ValueError("autoMode in %s is not an object" % path)
    if "allow" not in auto:
        return data, "no allow"
    allow = auto["allow"]
    if not isinstance(allow, list):
        raise ValueError("autoMode.allow in %s is not a list" % path)
    if RULE in allow:
        return data, "standing"
    if any(e in allow for e in EARLIER):
        return data, "earlier"
    return data, "absent"


def write_file(path, data, existed):
    # A link is followed to the file it points to, and the link itself stays.
    target = os.path.realpath(path)
    d = os.path.dirname(target)
    if os.path.islink(path) and not os.path.isdir(d):
        raise OSError("the link %s points to %s, whose directory does not "
                      "exist, and this program makes none there"
                      % (path, target))
    os.makedirs(d, exist_ok=True)
    fd, tmp = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=".settings.json.", dir=d)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            json.dump(data, f, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)
            f.write("\n")
            f.flush()
            os.fsync(f.fileno())
        if existed:
            shutil.copymode(target, tmp)
        os.replace(tmp, target)
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise


def main(argv):
    if argv[1:] == ["--self-test"]:
        return selftest()
    if len(argv) != 2 or argv[1] not in ("read", "write"):
        print("devloop-auto-mode-rule: expected one argument, read, write or "
              "--self-test", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    path = settings_path()
    try:
        data, state = load(path)
    except (ValueError, OSError) as e:
        if argv[1] == "read":
            print("the settings file cannot be read for the rule: %s" % e)
        else:
            print("not written: %s" % e)
        return 2
    if argv[1] == "read":
        if state == "standing":
            print("the rule stands in %s" % path)
            return 0
        why = {
            "missing": "the file does not exist",
            "no autoMode": "the file holds no autoMode",
            "no allow": "autoMode holds no allow list",
            "earlier": "the list autoMode.allow carries an earlier wording of "
                       "it, which allows less",
            "absent": "the list autoMode.allow does not carry it",
        }[state]
        print("the rule does not stand in %s: %s" % (path, why))
        return 1
    if state == "standing":
        print("the rule stands in %s already; nothing changed" % path)
        return 0
    if data is None:
        data = {}
    if state in ("missing", "no autoMode"):
        data["autoMode"] = {"allow": [DEFAULTS, RULE]}
        how = 'the list autoMode.allow created, with "$defaults" before the rule'
    elif state == "no allow":
        data["autoMode"]["allow"] = [DEFAULTS, RULE]
        how = 'the list autoMode.allow created, with "$defaults" before the rule'
    elif state == "earlier":
        allow = data["autoMode"]["allow"]
        first = min(i for i, e in enumerate(allow) if e in EARLIER)
        allow[first] = RULE
        allow[first + 1:] = [e for e in allow[first + 1:] if e not in EARLIER]
        how = "the earlier wording replaced in its place in the list autoMode.allow"
    else:
        data["autoMode"]["allow"].append(RULE)
        how = "appended to the list autoMode.allow"
    try:
        write_file(path, data, state != "missing")
    except OSError as e:
        print("not written: the write was refused: %s" % e)
        return 2
    print("the rule is entered in %s: %s" % (path, how))
    return 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------- the ways

def rule_ways(rule=RULE):
    """The ways the rule names, as (tools, browsers): the commands of its list
    between "by these commands: " and " for a browser.", the command-line
    tools before ", and ", the browser installers behind it."""
    m = re.search(r"by these commands: (.*) for a browser\.", rule)
    if m is None:
        return [], []
    tools, _, browsers = m.group(1).partition(", and ")
    return re.split(r", | and ", tools), re.split(r", | or ", browsers)


def guard_source(path=GUARD):
    """The code lines of the guard: every line whose first character other
    than a blank is not #."""
    with open(path, encoding="utf-8") as f:
        return [l for l in f.read().split("\n") if not l.lstrip().startswith("#")]


def guard_ways(lines):
    """What the source of the guard reads, as (routes, browsers, counted): the
    routes whose place it asks on the machine, place NAME; the browser
    installers it reads a download of, BROWSER=NAME; and the ways it records
    a refusal by, way "WAY"."""
    text = "\n".join(lines)
    return (sorted(set(re.findall(r'\bplace ([a-z]+) "', text))),
            sorted(set(re.findall(r'\bBROWSER=([a-z]+)\b', text))),
            sorted(set(re.findall(r'\bway "([^"$]+)"', text))))


# A command per way, the spelling the rule writes, and the three further forms
# the guard reads as pip; each is fed to the guard under PermissionDenied.
WAY_COMMANDS = (
    ("brew install", "brew install devloop-probe"),
    ("go install", "go install example.com/devloop-probe@latest"),
    ("npm install -g", "npm install -g devloop-probe"),
    ("pnpm add -g", "pnpm add -g devloop-probe"),
    ("yarn global add", "yarn global add devloop-probe"),
    ("bun add -g", "bun add -g devloop-probe"),
    ("cargo install", "cargo install devloop-probe"),
    ("gem install", "gem install devloop-probe"),
    ("pipx install", "pipx install devloop-probe"),
    ("uv tool install", "uv tool install devloop-probe"),
    ("pip install", "pip install devloop-probe"),
    ("pip install", "pip3 install devloop-probe"),
    ("pip install", "python3 -m pip install devloop-probe"),
    ("pip install", "uv pip install devloop-probe"),
    ("playwright install", "npx playwright install chromium"),
    ("cypress install", "npx cypress install"),
    ("puppeteer browsers install", "npx puppeteer browsers install chrome"),
)


# ------------------------------------------------------------- the self-test

def selftest():
    me = os.path.realpath(__file__)
    # the real path, since /var is a link to /private/var on macOS
    T = os.path.realpath(tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="devloop-auto-mode-rule."))
    n = [0]
    try:
        config = os.path.join(T, "config")
        settings = os.path.join(config, "settings.json")
        env = dict(os.environ)
        env["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = config

        def fresh(content=None):
            for p in (settings, os.path.join(T, "elsewhere")):
                if os.path.islink(p) or os.path.isfile(p):
                    os.unlink(p)
                elif os.path.isdir(p):
                    shutil.rmtree(p)
            os.makedirs(config, exist_ok=True)
            if content is not None:
                with open(settings, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                    f.write(content if isinstance(content, str) else json.dumps(content, indent=2))

        def run(*args, config_dir=None):
            e = dict(env)
            if config_dir is not None:
                e["CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"] = config_dir
            r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, me] + list(args), cwd=T,
                               capture_output=True, text=True, env=e)
            return r.returncode, r.stdout + r.stderr

        def check(name, cond, shown=""):
            n[0] += 1
            if cond:
                print("ok   %s" % name)
                return True
            print("FAIL %s" % name)
            if shown:
                print("    " + "\n    ".join(shown.split("\n")))
            return False

        def expect(name, got, want_rc, *want):
            rc, out = got
            n[0] += 1
            if rc != want_rc:
                print("FAIL %s: exit %d, expected %d" % (name, rc, want_rc))
                print("    " + "\n    ".join(out.split("\n")))
                return False
            for w in want:
                if w not in out:
                    print("FAIL %s: %r is not printed" % (name, w))
                    print("    " + "\n    ".join(out.split("\n")))
                    return False
            print("ok   %s" % name)
            return True

        def content(p=settings):
            with open(p, encoding="utf-8") as f:
                return f.read()

        def parsed(p=settings):
            return json.loads(content(p))

        ok = True
        old = EARLIER[0]
        # the arguments
        ok &= expect("no argument: exit 2", run(), 2, "expected one argument, read, write or --self-test")
        ok &= expect("an argument it does not know: exit 2", run("delete"), 2, "expected one argument")
        ok &= expect("two arguments: exit 2", run("read", "write"), 2, "expected one argument")
        # read, each answer
        fresh()
        ok &= expect("read, no file: exit 1, the file does not exist", run("read"), 1,
                     "the rule does not stand in %s: the file does not exist" % settings)
        fresh({"theme": "dark"})
        ok &= expect("read, no autoMode: exit 1", run("read"), 1, "the file holds no autoMode")
        fresh({"autoMode": {"deny": []}})
        ok &= expect("read, no allow list: exit 1", run("read"), 1, "autoMode holds no allow list")
        fresh({"autoMode": {"allow": [DEFAULTS, "something else"]}})
        ok &= expect("read, a list without the rule: exit 1", run("read"), 1,
                     "the list autoMode.allow does not carry it")
        fresh({"autoMode": {"allow": [DEFAULTS, old]}})
        ok &= expect("read, the earlier wording standing: exit 1, it allows less", run("read"), 1,
                     "the list autoMode.allow carries an earlier wording of it, which allows less")
        fresh({"autoMode": {"allow": [DEFAULTS, RULE]}})
        ok &= expect("read, the rule standing: exit 0", run("read"), 0, "the rule stands in %s" % settings)
        fresh({"autoMode": {"allow": [old, RULE]}})
        ok &= expect("read, the rule and the earlier wording both standing: exit 0", run("read"), 0, "the rule stands in")
        fresh("{ not json\n")
        ok &= expect("read, no JSON: exit 2", run("read"), 2, "the settings file cannot be read for the rule:", "is not valid JSON")
        fresh('{"a": 1, "a": 2}\n')
        ok &= expect("read, a key twice: exit 2", run("read"), 2, "the key 'a' stands twice in one object")
        fresh("[]\n")
        ok &= expect("read, no object at the top: exit 2", run("read"), 2, "does not hold an object at its top")
        fresh({"autoMode": "on"})
        ok &= expect("read, autoMode no object: exit 2", run("read"), 2, "autoMode in %s is not an object" % settings)
        fresh({"autoMode": {"allow": "x"}})
        ok &= expect("read, allow no list: exit 2", run("read"), 2, "autoMode.allow in %s is not a list" % settings)
        fresh()
        os.makedirs(settings)
        ok &= expect("read, settings.json a directory: exit 2", run("read"), 2, "the settings file cannot be read for the rule:")
        # write, each answer
        fresh()
        ok &= expect("write, no file: the list created, exit 0", run("write"), 0,
                     'the rule is entered in %s: the list autoMode.allow created, with "$defaults" before the rule' % settings)
        ok &= check("write, no file: the file holds the list and nothing else",
                    parsed() == {"autoMode": {"allow": [DEFAULTS, RULE]}}, content())
        fresh('{\n  "theme": "dark",\n  "env": {"A": "1"}\n}\n')
        ok &= expect("write, no autoMode: the list created", run("write"), 0, "the list autoMode.allow created")
        ok &= check("write, no autoMode: every other key kept, in its order",
                    list(parsed().items()) == [("theme", "dark"), ("env", {"A": "1"}), ("autoMode", {"allow": [DEFAULTS, RULE]})], content())
        fresh({"autoMode": {"deny": ["x"]}})
        ok &= expect("write, no allow list: the list created", run("write"), 0, "the list autoMode.allow created")
        ok &= check("write, no allow list: deny kept", parsed() == {"autoMode": {"deny": ["x"], "allow": [DEFAULTS, RULE]}}, content())
        fresh({"autoMode": {"allow": ["mine"]}})
        ok &= expect("write, a list standing: appended, exit 0", run("write"), 0, "appended to the list autoMode.allow")
        ok &= check("write, a list standing: no $defaults added", parsed()["autoMode"]["allow"] == ["mine", RULE], content())
        fresh({"theme": "dark", "autoMode": {"allow": [DEFAULTS, old, "mine"]}})
        ok &= expect("write, the earlier wording standing: replaced in its place, exit 0", run("write"), 0,
                     "the earlier wording replaced in its place in the list autoMode.allow")
        ok &= check("write, the earlier wording standing: the rule at its place, no second beside it",
                    parsed() == {"theme": "dark", "autoMode": {"allow": [DEFAULTS, RULE, "mine"]}}, content())
        fresh({"autoMode": {"allow": ["mine", old, "other", old]}})
        run("write")
        ok &= check("write, the earlier wording twice: the first replaced, the second gone",
                    parsed()["autoMode"]["allow"] == ["mine", RULE, "other"], content())
        ok &= expect("read after the replacement: exit 0", run("read"), 0, "the rule stands in")
        fresh({"autoMode": {"allow": [DEFAULTS, RULE]}})
        before = content()
        ok &= expect("write, the rule standing: nothing changed, exit 0", run("write"), 0, "already; nothing changed")
        ok &= check("write, the rule standing: the file byte for byte as before", content() == before)
        for name, text, want in (("no JSON", "{ not json\n", "is not valid JSON"),
                                 ("a key twice", '{"a": 1, "a": 2}\n', "stands twice"),
                                 ("autoMode no object", '{"autoMode": 1}\n', "is not an object"),
                                 ("allow no list", '{"autoMode": {"allow": {}}}\n', "is not a list")):
            fresh(text)
            ok &= expect("write, %s: not written, exit 2" % name, run("write"), 2, "not written: ", want)
            ok &= check("write, %s: the file as before" % name, content() == text)
        # the write refused: the configuration directory lies under a file
        blocker = os.path.join(T, "a-file")
        with open(blocker, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
            f.write("x\n")
        ok &= expect("write, the directory cannot be made: the write refused, exit 2",
                     run("write", config_dir=os.path.join(blocker, "config")), 2, "not written: the write was refused:")
        ok &= check("write refused: nothing made", os.path.isfile(blocker) and content(blocker) == "x\n")
        # the permissions of the file kept
        if os.name == "nt":
            print("skip the permissions of the file: Windows keeps no mode bits for this program to keep")
        else:
            fresh({"theme": "dark"})
            os.chmod(settings, 0o600)
            run("write")
            ok &= check("write: the file keeps its permissions, 600",
                        stat.S_IMODE(os.stat(settings).st_mode) == 0o600, oct(os.stat(settings).st_mode))
        ok &= check("write: no temporary file left beside the file",
                    not [x for x in os.listdir(config) if x.startswith(".settings.json.")], " ".join(os.listdir(config)))
        # a hard link: the other name keeps the old content
        fresh({"theme": "dark"})
        other = os.path.join(T, "other-name.json")
        if os.path.exists(other):
            os.unlink(other)
        try:
            os.link(settings, other)
        except (OSError, AttributeError) as e:
            print("skip a hard link: this system makes none here: %s" % e)
        else:
            run("write")
            ok &= check("write through a hard link: the other name keeps the old content",
                        json.loads(content(other)) == {"theme": "dark"} and parsed()["autoMode"]["allow"] == [DEFAULTS, RULE])
        # symbolic links, where the system makes one
        fresh()
        elsewhere = os.path.join(T, "elsewhere")
        os.makedirs(elsewhere)
        target = os.path.join(elsewhere, "settings.json")
        try:
            os.symlink(target, settings)
        except (OSError, NotImplementedError, AttributeError) as e:
            print("skip symbolic links: this system makes none here: %s" % e)
        else:
            with open(target, "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
                json.dump({"theme": "dark"}, f)
            ok &= expect("write through a link: exit 0", run("write"), 0, "the list autoMode.allow created")
            ok &= check("write through a link: the link stays and the file it points to is written",
                        os.path.islink(settings) and json.loads(content(target))["autoMode"]["allow"] == [DEFAULTS, RULE])
            ok &= expect("read through a link: exit 0", run("read"), 0, "the rule stands in")
            os.unlink(target)
            ok &= expect("write through a link to a missing file: the file made there, exit 0", run("write"), 0,
                         "the list autoMode.allow created")
            ok &= check("a link to a missing file: the link stays and the file stands where it points",
                        os.path.islink(settings) and os.path.isfile(target))
            os.unlink(settings)
            shutil.rmtree(elsewhere)
            os.symlink(target, settings)
            ok &= expect("write through a link into a missing directory: not written, exit 2", run("write"), 2,
                         "not written: the write was refused:", "whose directory does not exist")
            ok &= check("a link into a missing directory: no directory made", not os.path.exists(elsewhere))
            os.unlink(settings)
        # the ways: the rule, the guard's source, the guard's record
        tools, browsers = rule_ways()
        routes, readers, counted = guard_ways(guard_source())
        ok &= check("the rule names fourteen ways, eleven tools and three browser installers: %s; %s"
                    % (", ".join(tools), ", ".join(browsers)), len(tools) == 11 and len(browsers) == 3)
        ok &= check("the routes the guard asks the place of are the tools of the rule: %s" % " ".join(routes),
                    routes == sorted(w.split()[0] for w in tools))
        ok &= check("the browser installers the guard reads are those of the rule: %s" % " ".join(readers),
                    readers == sorted(w.split()[0] for w in browsers))
        ok &= check("the ways the guard records a refusal by are the ways of the rule",
                    counted == sorted(tools + browsers), "guard: %r\nrule:  %r" % (counted, sorted(tools + browsers)))
        ok &= check("every way of the rule has a command below", sorted(set(w for w, _ in WAY_COMMANDS)) == sorted(tools + browsers))
        bash = shutil.which("bash")
        if bash is None:
            ok &= check("bash found on PATH, to run the install guard", False)
        else:
            def posix(p):
                return p.replace("\\", "/")
            p = os.path.join(T, "project")
            os.makedirs(os.path.join(p, "docs", "agents"))
            subprocess.run(["git", "init", "-q", p], check=True)
            record_file = os.path.join(p, ".git", "devloop-auto-mode-denials")
            genv = dict(env)
            genv["CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR"] = posix(p)
            recorded = set()
            for i, (way, command) in enumerate(WAY_COMMANDS):
                if os.path.exists(record_file):
                    os.unlink(record_file)
                denial = {"session_id": "s1", "cwd": posix(p), "hook_event_name": "PermissionDenied",
                          "permission_mode": "auto", "tool_name": "Bash", "tool_use_id": "toolu_way%d" % i,
                          "tool_input": {"command": command, "description": "install"},
                          "reason": "The server-side auto mode classifier judged this action dangerous (it gave no explanation)"}
                r = subprocess.run([bash, posix(GUARD)], cwd=p, input=json.dumps(denial),
                                   capture_output=True, text=True, env=genv)
                lines = []
                if os.path.exists(record_file):
                    with open(record_file, encoding="utf-8") as f:
                        lines = [l.rstrip("\r\n").split("\t") for l in f if l.strip()]
                good = r.returncode == 0 and len(lines) == 1 and len(lines[0]) == 4 and lines[0][1] == way and lines[0][3] == command
                if good:
                    recorded.add(way)
                ok &= check("the guard records a refusal of %s as the way %s" % (command, way), good,
                            "exit %d, %s holds %r\n%s" % (r.returncode, record_file, lines, r.stdout + r.stderr))
            ok &= check("the ways the guard records are the ways of the rule", sorted(recorded) == sorted(tools + browsers))
        if ok:
            print("SELF-TEST PASSED: %d cases" % n[0])
            return 0
        print("SELF-TEST FAILED")
        return 1
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(T, ignore_errors=True)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
